{:edb/db-before-t 1233 :edb/db-after-t 1234 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534546 50 #inst "2026-10-07T22:05:07.904Z" 13194139534546 true] [17592186171892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294091 13194139534546 true] [17592186171892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294106 13194139534546 true] [17592186171892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294113 13194139534546 true] [17592186171952 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294098 13194139534546 true] [17592186172482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534546 true] [17592186172482 1100 "Solve the inequality $((1 - x)(x - 2))/(x + 5) \\le 0$." 13194139534546 false] [17592186172482 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(1-x)(x-2)}{x+5}\\le 0.$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186172482 1145 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(1-x)(x-2)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,1),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-5,1]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294091 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294092 13194139534546 true] [17592186294091 1089 "q-115979" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294091 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534546 true] [17592186294091 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{4-x}\\ge 0.$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294091 1113 #uuid "04188be1-dec4-549b-9e2d-e556e183375a" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294091 1145 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{4-x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+1)}{4-x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=4.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $4-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(0,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294093 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294094 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294095 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294096 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294097 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1041 "selection" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294097 13194139534546 true] [17592186294092 1110 #uuid "67d5cdc8-0080-50bd-a2b3-565c9bbe5455" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294093 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294093 1111 #uuid "68959728-df6d-5aa8-813d-2349b4a9960d" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294094 1034 "x\\in [0,4]" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294094 1111 #uuid "1b580835-b73c-5ada-bcd2-267099de927c" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294095 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [4,\\infty )" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294095 1111 #uuid "1c5a976c-7776-56fa-88d0-fb30210ac2d3" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294096 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup (4,\\infty )" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294096 1111 #uuid "37032f17-d569-512e-bbd4-d80a588481f5" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294097 1034 "x\\in [0,4)" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294097 1111 #uuid "2d780ba5-14b2-5e9f-8d6c-77d902c2f74e" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294099 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294102 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294104 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1089 "q-274430" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1100 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{(x-2)(x-1)}{x+3}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty ).$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1113 #uuid "0a1905e1-52c0-58f8-8390-d9d1a449a196" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294098 1145 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x-1)}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-3.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,1),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)>0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (-3,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294100 13194139534546 true] [17592186294099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294101 13194139534546 true] [17592186294099 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534546 true] [17592186294099 1041 "field-1" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294099 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294101 13194139534546 true] [17592186294099 1110 #uuid "b007b3c2-ada5-5054-b3f3-3f58c23ae314" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294100 1034 "3" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294100 1111 #uuid "31a394a4-3b3e-53ba-b609-8850195dd65b" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294101 1034 "-3" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294101 1111 #uuid "a6cefdd0-7ef3-5ef1-b056-1579c453f187" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294103 13194139534546 true] [17592186294102 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534546 true] [17592186294102 1041 "field-2" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294102 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294103 13194139534546 true] [17592186294102 1110 #uuid "70851315-cfec-51b6-8150-def7181e0ec8" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294103 1034 "1" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294103 1111 #uuid "39892fbf-8380-5b0b-963c-3615780aaced" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294104 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294105 13194139534546 true] [17592186294104 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534546 true] [17592186294104 1041 "field-3" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294104 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294105 13194139534546 true] [17592186294104 1110 #uuid "9716469c-c1da-55a5-89c6-4983a3f905e3" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294105 1034 "2" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294105 1111 #uuid "43a0231e-16a0-5768-a630-7d0f91003665" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294107 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294109 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294111 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1089 "q-326141" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1100 "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1113 #uuid "ba6880bf-2980-5277-ba16-e7a358e780ef" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294106 1145 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-3)(x+1)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=-1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive  on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-5,-1\\,]⋃[\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294108 13194139534546 true] [17592186294107 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534546 true] [17592186294107 1041 "field-1" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294107 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294108 13194139534546 true] [17592186294107 1110 #uuid "92cb2bbb-1d54-513b-8367-a72dde7ffe8b" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294108 1034 "-5" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294108 1111 #uuid "01e70649-c311-5b0f-ab2c-a9e78495fa07" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294110 13194139534546 true] [17592186294109 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534546 true] [17592186294109 1041 "field-2" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294109 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294110 13194139534546 true] [17592186294109 1110 #uuid "02747366-9c88-5585-8e70-aa65238eaa87" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294110 1034 "-1" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534546 true] [17592186294110 1111 #uuid "6ffe037c-3d23-516f-b0ec-2041d731ccd8" 13194139534546 true] [17592186294111 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294112 131941